    feat(web): adapt auth CTA to sign in vs sign up for returning vs new visitors

    Logged-out visitors now get the right affordance instead of always "Sign in":
    a browser that has completed a login before sees "Sign in" / "Welcome back",
    a likely-new visitor sees "Sign up" / "Create your account". Logged-in users
    are unaffected (still redirected to /dashboard).

    A first-party `ac_returning` hint cookie, set on the WorkOS auth callback's
    onSuccess and read server-side by isReturningVisitor(), drives the choice. It
    is a non-sensitive UX hint, not auth — WorkOS still fully enforces sessions.
    An explicit ?mode=signin|signup always overrides the cookie.

    - /auth/login is dual-mode (getSignInUrl / getSignUpUrl) with adaptive copy, a
      sign-in/up toggle that preserves returnTo, and it surfaces ?error=callback_failed.
    - The homepage header/hero and the shared MarketingHeader render one AuthCtaLink
      / authCta() helper so the CTA can't drift between surfaces.
    - The hint cookie's `secure` flag derives from the redirect-URI protocol, matching
      the WorkOS session cookie.

